messages.reasoning_content:
Now the integration test side: `src/entrypoints/machinery/reconcile_run_ending.test.ts`.

Changes:
1. `argv()` helper: support `run-event` override (default: absent — no, hmm. Let me think).

Current helper:

```ts
function argv(overrides: Record<string, string> = {}): string[] {
  return [
    "--display-title", overrides["display-title"] ?? `${AGENT} on #${NUMBER}`,
    "--run-started-at", overrides["run-started-at"] ?? START,
    "--run-url", overrides["run-url"] ?? RUN_URL,
    ...(overrides.conclusion === undefined ? [] : ["--conclusion", overrides.conclusion]),
  ];
}
```

Add:
```ts
    ...(overrides["run-event"] === undefined ? [] : ["--run-event", overrides["run-event"]]),
```

2. Entry-run tests: name the event.

- "an entry run that dispatched inside its own lifetime gets the notice, naming the thread's agent" — add `"run-event": "issues"` (an entry run a new issue started, whose body named the agent — realistic, and pins that the marker branch is ungated). 
- "an entry run that died before any marker still gets the notice, off the person's command" — add `"run-event": "issue_comment"` (the command's own run).
- "an entry run that took no turn stays silent" — add `"run-event": "issue_comment"` (marker at BEFORE is an older run's; silent either way).
- "an entry run over an untouched thread stays silent" — add `"run-event": "issues"` (realistic: a remark on a fresh issue).

3. New test — the #281 incident, both directions:

```ts
test("an entry run a new issue started stays silent on the command its OWN run is working on (#281)", () => {
  // The incident: the issue opened, the entry run completed, and three seconds in
  // the person commented. The command's own run (`issue_comment`) was still
  // working — the notice claimed it. Same thread, two events: the comment's run
  // gets the notice when IT dies, the issue's run never does.
  const command = apiComment("/atomaton\nplease look", AFTER, true);
  const issueRun = run(argv({ "display-title": `#${NUMBER}`, "run-event": "issues" }), rulesFor({ comments: [command] }));
  expect(issueRun.status).toBe(0);
  expect(issueRun.ghCalls.filter((argv) => argv.includes("comment"))).toEqual([]);

  const commentRun = run(argv({ "display-title": `#${NUMBER}`, "run-event": "issue_comment" }), rulesFor({ comments: [command] }));
  expect(commentRun.status).toBe(0);
  expect(postedNotice(commentRun)).toContain("atomaton");
  expect(commentRun.ghCalls.some((argv) => argv.includes("--remove-label"))).toBe(true);
});
```

Hmm wait — in this test the command is at AFTER (2026-10-23) which is AFTER START (the run start 2026-10-22T10:00). For the `issue_comment` variant, tookTurn's human branch requires `at < start` — command at AFTER is AFTER the run start → false → silent! That contradicts what I wrote. Let me redo the incident's timings for the test:

The incident: entry run started 13:25:48, completed 13:25:59; command posted 13:25:51 (after the entry run started, before it completed); the command's run started 13:25:48-ish... no wait. Hmm — the command's run started AFTER the command. The watch fired for the ENTRY run (started 13:25:48). Command at 13:25:51 is after the entry run's START. For the entry run: flag false → silent regardless of time. ✓.

For the comment's OWN run: its start is ~13:25:51+ (after the command). So in the mirror test, the run-started-at should be AFTER the command time. So the two variants need DIFFERENT `--run-started-at` values: the entry run's start (before the command) for the incident, the comment run's start (after the command) for the mirror. 

So:

```ts
const command = apiComment("/atomaton\nplease look", DURING, true); // DURING = 2026-10-22T11:00, START = 10:00
// entry run started BEFORE the command (START), command at DURING:
const issueRun = run(argv({ "display-title": `#${NUMBER}`, "run-event": "issues" }), rulesFor({ comments: [command] }));
// → silent.

// the command's own run started AFTER the command:
const commentRun = run(argv({ "display-title": `#${NUMBER}`, "run-started-at": LATER, "run-event": "issue_comment" }), rulesFor({ comments: [command] }));
// → notice.
```

where LATER = "2026-10-24T09:00:00Z" (AFTER). Actually AFTER is already defined as "2026-10-23T09:00:00Z". So mirror uses `run-started-at: AFTER`.

Wait — there's already a test "an entry run that died before any marker still gets the notice, off the person's command" with command at BEFORE (before START) and run-event issue_comment → that covers "command before start → notify" in the script flow. The mirror in my new test would duplicate it — but with the command at DURING (mid-run) and a start AFTER it, which is the realistic shape of the comment's own run (the command precedes the run start). Hmm, actually both are the same assertion shape: command before run start + issue_comment → notify. The distinguishing bit of the incident is the entry-run side (issues → silent). 

Let me simplify: the new integration test covers the incident with both variants where the timings are the incident's:

- Entry run: `--run-started-at START` (13:25:48-ish), command at DURING (after START). Event `issues` → SILENT. ✓ (the bug fix)
- The command's run: `--run-started-at AFTER` (after the command at DURING... wait DURING=2026-10-22T11:00, AFTER=2026-10-23T09:00 — yes AFTER > DURING). Event `issue_comment` → NOTICE. ✓

That one test then proves both: the fix silences the entry run, and the same thread still notifies when the command's own run dies. It's "1本" (one test) covering the `--run-event` pass-through both ways. 

But then "an entry run that died before any marker still gets the notice, off the person's command" — update it to pass `"run-event": "issue_comment"`. It stays as the classic manual-comment death (command at BEFORE, i.e., before that run's start — the normal order).

Hmm — one moment. In that existing test, command at BEFORE = 2026-10-20, run start 2026-10-22T10:00. Command is 2 days before the run start. Realistic? The comment starts the run, so the run starts seconds after — but for the gate only the order matters (before → notify). Fine.

4. Also update the file's top docstring? It says "what is pinned here is the FLOW". Fine as is.

Also — should the `rulesFor` `--jq` rule (mostRecentAgentOn) matter for the new test? The notice path calls `mostRecentAgentOn(repo, number)` which reads bodies via `--jq` → returns MARKER + "/engineer continue here" → newest is "/engineer continue here"... wait — mostRecentAgentOn walks newest-first; the rule returns `[MARKER, "/engineer continue here"]` — hmm, order: is that oldest-first or newest-first? The rule's stdout is `["<!-- atomaton:dispatch=engineer -->", "/engineer continue here"]`. If oldest-first, newest is "/engineer continue here" → agent "engineer". The existing dead-before-marker test expects the notice to contain AGENT ("engineer") — consistent with oldest-first. OK so for my new test with `--run-event issue_comment`, the notice's agent = "engineer" (from the jq rule). The command in the thread is `/atomaton` — but mostRecentAgentOn reads the FAKE jq response, so the notice names "engineer". That's fine but a bit confusing; the assertion should just be that a notice was posted. Let me assert `postedNotice(commentRun)).toBeDefined()` rather than a specific agent. Hmm, or contain "ended without posting" — the notice text. Let me assert toBeDefined() and that the label removal happened.

Wait — for the label removal: `releasesLabel(lastEndingComment(...))` — thread comments = [command] only; no ending → releasesLabel(undefined) → false → label STAYS. So `--remove-label` NOT called. Right — a thread with only a command has no ending, so the label isn't touched. So don't assert label removal in the mirror.

Hmm wait, but hold on — the notice is posted, but the label stays on? That's the current behaviour for that shape (thread has no readable ending → "No readable ending holds the label: a thread this rule cannot read is not a thread to act on from outside" — releasesLabel false). Hmm wait, that reads backwards — "No readable ending holds the label" means releasesLabel returns FALSE, so the label STAYS. The docstring at line 220-221 says: "No readable ending holds the label: a thread this rule cannot read is not a thread to act on from outside." — so when there's no ending, the label is kept. OK so my new test: no `--remove-label`. ✓ matches rulesFor with comments=[command].

Now — one more consideration in the new test for the `issues` variant: `requestOutstanding(thread.events)` — the command IS an ask (newest surviving event = asked) → true. Then `tookTurn(...)` → false (flag) → endingMissing false → silent, no `gh` comment call, and NO label work either (returns before label read... actually returns at `if (!endingMissing) return;` — before `hasLabel`). So ghCalls = [] entirely? The rules include an `--jq` rule for comments... wait, the read of comments happens BEFORE the gate: `readRunWatchThread` reads comments first. So ghCalls will contain the comments read (api call) but no "comment" (posting) call. The assertion `r.ghCalls.filter((argv) => argv.includes("comment"))` — hmm, "comment" appears in the api URL path `repos/o/r/issues/7/comments`! The existing tests use this filter for "posted a notice" — `ghCalls.filter((argv) => argv.includes("comment"))` would match the api read too... Let me check how ghCalls are recorded — maybe the argv for the api read is `["api", "repos/o/r/issues/7/comments"]` which CONTAINS "comment" as substring ("comments" contains "comment"). Hmm! But the existing tests use exactly this filter and expect `[]` for silent cases — e.g. "a run that posted its ending leaves the thread alone" expects `r.ghCalls.filter((argv) => argv.includes("comment"))).toEqual([])` — but the thread WAS read (comments api). If the read matched, the filter would be non-empty. So either the fake gh records calls differently, or the api read path doesn't contain "comment"... it does — "comments". Unless `ghCalls` records only... hmm wait, let me look at the harness to be sure.

Actually wait — look at test "a title that names no node does nothing at all, not even the read": `expect(r.ghCalls, title).toEqual([])` — so ghCalls WOULD include the read if it happened; the title check returns before any read. And "an entry run that took no turn stays silent" expects `filter(...includes("comment"))).toEqual([])` — but the thread must have been READ for that test (the gate needs the thread). The read is `gh api repos/o/r/issues/7/comments`... which includes "comment". So `ghCalls.filter(argv => argv.includes("comment"))` would be non-empty → test would fail?! Unless `ghCalls` stores argv differently... Let me read the harness.

